# Run `./run.sh setup` to create `.env` from this file (or copy it by hand),
# then fill in real values before starting a workshop.
# `.env` is gitignored — never commit real workshop credentials.
#
# This file holds account/secret/network settings only — the same for every
# workshop. Which workshop to run (content, Forgejo org/repo, any Compose
# overlay) now lives in workshops/<name>/workshop.env and is picked by:
#
#   ./run.sh <workshop-name>
#
# The defaults below are only used if you run `docker compose up` directly
# instead of `./run.sh` (e.g. for quick iteration on the engine itself).

# --- Which workshop content to serve -----------------------------------
# Path to a workshops/<name>/content directory (relative to this file).
# Normally set by workshops/<name>/workshop.env via ./run.sh instead.
WORKSHOP_CONTENT_DIR=../workshops/git-fundamentals/content
WORKSHOP_NAME="Git Training Lab"

# --- Public address --------------------------------------------------------
# What students type into their browser. Everything (terminal, Forgejo,
# slides) is served from this one origin through the gateway.
#
# Local testing (no TLS, no DNS needed; the port must match GATEWAY_HTTP_PORT):
#   PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
# Azure VM (free per-IP DNS label; Caddy gets a real cert automatically —
# no custom domain needed):
#   PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://<your-label>.<region>.cloudapp.azure.com
# A LAN class: use https (run.sh warns about http:// beyond localhost, since
# logins and terminal input would cross the network in the clear). See
# engine/README.md, "LAN class over HTTPS". With https the gateway also
# sends HSTS.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
# Behind another TLS proxy (e.g. a home-lab Caddy): the gateway serves plain
# HTTP here while PUBLIC_BASE_URL stays the https address browsers use, and
# GATEWAY_TRUSTED_PROXIES names that proxy, as the gateway sees it, so the
# login rate limit counts each browser, not the proxy. Only the proxy's
# address, never a whole private range. Usually set in .env.<name> (--env).
#   GATEWAY_LISTEN=http://:8080
#   GATEWAY_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.2/32
#
# Default passwords (student, change-me, ...) on a non-loopback PUBLIC_BASE_URL are refused unless
# --allow-default-passwords is passed. For a lab only reachable over a VPN, set this in .env.<name>
# instead of typing the flag every time:
#   ALLOW_DEFAULT_PASSWORDS=1

# The gateway's private network to the students' IDE and terminal ports
# (terminal_ingress). Change it only if 172.30.9.0/24 clashes with a network
# on this host.
#   TERMINAL_INGRESS_SUBNET=172.30.9.0/24

# --- Host networking ------------------------------------------------------
# Interface the gateway binds to on THIS machine. 127.0.0.1 = this machine
# only. On a VM this is normally 0.0.0.0 (0.0.0.0 is safe here — the real
# access boundary on a VM should be the cloud NSG/firewall, scoped to your
# corporate network, not this setting).
LAB_HOST_IP=127.0.0.1
# Ports the gateway publishes. 8080/8443 because rootless podman can't bind
# ports below 1024; put a proxy or port mapping in front for 80/443.
GATEWAY_HTTP_PORT=8080
GATEWAY_HTTPS_PORT=8443

# --- Shared browser gate ----------------------------------------------
# The class login on the /login page. It guards the terminal, Forgejo and
# slides. Wrong guesses are rate-limited: 30 a minute per client, then 429.
TTYD_USERNAME=learner
TTYD_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Student accounts -------------------------------------------------
# Linux terminal accounts and matching Forgejo accounts (student01 through
# studentNN). Linux passwords are locked (students never type one). Each
# Forgejo password is derived from STUDENT_PASSWORD_SEED (remediation
# T2.1b): nobody types those either, since git uses a per-student token the
# terminal writes to ~/.git-credentials and the Forgejo card signs in by
# itself. The /admin Roster shows a student's password when needed.
# Generate a real random value, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
STUDENT_COUNT=20
STUDENT_PREFIX=student
STUDENT_PASSWORD_SEED=change-me
# Only for an older engine/.env without STUDENT_PASSWORD_SEED: then every
# student's Forgejo password is this one. run.sh warns about it.
# STUDENT_PASSWORD=

# --- Achievements (optional) ------------------------------------------
# 1 adds the achievements module to any workshop that has a catalog
# (workshops/<name>/achievements/); 0 or unset loads nothing extra. The
# catalog is checked at start and a bad one stops the run. In progress: see
# ROADMAP.md, "Achievements".
ACHIEVEMENTS_ENABLED=0
# Tunables (these are the defaults; leave them commented to keep them). Funny unlocks are
# worth 0 and can never pay out; cheating costs at most 1 point.
# ACHIEVEMENTS_ANONYMOUS=1           leaderboard shows generated names (WildCrab), not accounts
# ACHIEVEMENTS_MILESTONE_POINTS=10
# ACHIEVEMENTS_FUNNY_POINTS=0
# ACHIEVEMENTS_CHALLENGE_POINTS=100
# ACHIEVEMENTS_CAPSTONE_POINTS=300
# ACHIEVEMENTS_HINT_PERCENT=25       each hint takes this % off a challenge's points (two count)
# ACHIEVEMENTS_COMPLETION_PERCENT=80 % of core milestones for "Workshop complete"
# ACHIEVEMENTS_FIRST_BLOOD=1         first student to clear a challenge gets a bonus
# ACHIEVEMENTS_CLASS_CLEAR=1         everyone gets a bonus once the whole class cleared one
# ACHIEVEMENTS_CHEAT_PENALTY=1       0 = cheating tiers still toast but cost nothing

# How fast the landing page hands out new slots: ASSIGN_BURST at once, then
# ASSIGN_PER_MINUTE (a class of 30 is in within about a minute). A browser
# that already has a slot, the facilitator and demo bots are never limited.
# ASSIGN_BURST=10
# ASSIGN_PER_MINUTE=20

# --- Demo/test bots (./run.sh <workshop> --test) -----------------------
# Simulated "students" (an expert, an intermediate, and a terminal/git
# novice persona -- see engine/web-terminal/bot-runner.sh) that slowly work
# through the git-fundamentals lab on their own, on testuserN/... branches,
# for demoing the workshop or exercising the facilitator dashboard without
# real students. Entirely separate from the STUDENT_* accounts above --
# bots never consume a student slot. BOT_COUNT=0 (the default) disables
# this feature; `--test` sets it to 3 for you unless you've already set it
# here, and `--test N` sets it to N (1-35) regardless. Bots 1-3 are the
# expert/intermediate/novice personas; any beyond that get a random one.
# Uncomment to change the bot accounts' Forgejo/Linux password from the
# default below.
# BOT_COUNT=0
# BOT_PREFIX=testuser
# BOT_PASSWORD=testuser123

# --- Web-terminal container resource ceiling ---------------------------
# WEB_TERMINAL_MEM_LIMIT/PIDS_LIMIT are the container-wide backstop for
# ALL concurrently-active code-server/ttyd processes combined
# (docker-compose.yml's web-terminal service). CODE_SERVER_MAX_HEAP_MB is
# a per-process safety net under that ceiling: the V8 heap cap on each
# node process a student's code-server runs (server, extension host, pty
# host, file watcher), passed as a node flag in workspace-control.py. It
# does NOT reach language servers (their extensions fork them with their
# own flags), so WEB_TERMINAL_MEM_LIMIT is the real ceiling for those.
#
# Before a real class, run this ON the target machine (the Azure VM
# itself, or your Mac for local testing) to get numbers sized to that
# machine's actual memory, ideally after `./run.sh <workshop> --test` so it
# can calibrate against real student processes instead of guessing:
#
#   ./run.sh capacity --students <STUDENT_COUNT above>
#
# Falling back to the rule of thumb by hand: budget ~650MB/student for RAM
# (set from ~480MB measured for a fresh connected session, plus headroom for
# terminals, git, and a longer session; now ~260MB since the extension trim
# and code-server node flags, so it's conservative -- see engine/README.md's
# Capacity section) and
# 40-50 pids/student for a sustained-activity session, both plus about 1GB
# fixed overhead. Or leave the defaults below for a small class.
WEB_TERMINAL_MEM_LIMIT=4g
WEB_TERMINAL_PIDS_LIMIT=2048
CODE_SERVER_MAX_HEAP_MB=384
# Per-student process limit (RLIMIT_NPROC, threads count): a fork-bomb
# tripwire, high enough that no normal work notices. 0 = off. Note
# WEB_TERMINAL_PIDS_LIMIT above still caps the whole container.
# TERMINAL_NPROC_LIMIT=1024

# How long a code-server lingers once its browser tab has closed. Frees
# memory from students who have left (break, end of class); never touches a
# student who still has the IDE open, however idle. GRACE kills the
# extension host + language servers (~330MB back per student; VS Code's
# own default is 3 HOURS), IDLE_TIMEOUT then exits the whole code-server
# (the next /ide visit starts it again, and terminals -- tmux sessions --
# survive). Keep GRACE below IDLE_TIMEOUT. 0 disables a timer; IDLE_TIMEOUT
# must otherwise be more than 60 (code-server refuses less, and the web-terminal
# container refuses to start with it).
CODE_SERVER_RECONNECTION_GRACE_SECONDS=300
CODE_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900

# --- Corporate CA bundle (only if your network does TLS inspection) --------
# web-terminal's build fetches a few pinned VS Code extensions over HTTPS
# from open-vsx.org (build time only -- the running container has no
# internet route, see docker-compose.yml). On a network with TLS
# inspection, that fetch fails with a cert-trust error unless it trusts the
# inspecting proxy's CA. Point this at whatever combined CA bundle your
# shell already uses for other HTTPS tools (curl/pip/etc); run.sh passes it
# to that one build step as a BuildKit secret, never baked into the image.
# Leave unset on an open network -- there's no effect either way.
# CORP_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/combined_ca_bundle.pem

# --- Facilitator account (Linux shell, sudo-capable) -----------------------
# Unlike the other workshop passwords above, this one grants passwordful
# sudo (see engine/web-terminal/entrypoint.sh) — treat it a step above
# "shared on a slide." Also gates /admin (the facilitator dashboard —
# roster/live-watch tiles/Release, see engine/allocator/), on top of the
# shared TTYD_USERNAME/PASSWORD gate.
FACILITATOR_USERNAME=facilitator
# Required -- entrypoint.sh now fails to start rather than default this to
# a guessable password.
FACILITATOR_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Control-plane shared secret --------------------------------------
# Machine-to-machine only -- never typed by a student or facilitator.
# Proves a start/stop/status request to web-terminal's internal control API
# (workspace-control.py) actually came from the allocator, not some other
# container reachable on the internal workshop_lab network (e.g. a
# workshop's own Forgejo Actions runner executing student-authored CI).
# Generate a real random value before a real workshop, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
CONTROL_TOKEN=change-me

# Proves a request to the allocator actually came through Caddy (gateway),
# not directly from some other container on the internal workshop_lab
# network (e.g. a student's own shell in web-terminal, forging X-Auth-User
# straight at http://allocator:8080/). Same required/random-value guidance
# as CONTROL_TOKEN above.
GATEWAY_TOKEN=change-me

# --- Forgejo admin account (created by the bootstrap service) --------------
FORGEJO_ADMIN_USER=workshop-admin
FORGEJO_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
FORGEJO_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# FORGEJO_ORG/FORGEJO_REPO: normally set by workshops/<name>/workshop.env via
# ./run.sh instead (each workshop can seed its own org/repo name).
FORGEJO_ORG=training
FORGEJO_REPO=sample-training-repo
